ay -0.5, so remaining this bright would rule out Ia decline."}, "weakest_point": "The fit pins x1 at -3.0 on the prior edge, requiring an extreme fast-decliner to explain a fairly slow post-peak decline."}, {"argument": "Rise from deep limits fainter than 20.3 mag at day -25 to a blue peak near day -9.3 with g-r near -0.1 mag matches a hot nuclear flare. Blackbody fit with T near 10800 K fits all detections with chi2 per point 0.7 and the real PSF-like detection has an underlying bright galaxy host.", "hypothesis": "tde", "refuted_if": {"band": "ztfg", "day": 3, "direction": "below", "flux": 100, "why": "TDE predicts a slow hot blue plateau near 150 microjansky at this phase, so a drop below 100 microjansky requires rapid fading and cooling incompatible with TDE emission."}, "weakest_point": "Stamp shows the transient offset from the bright galaxy core, violating the nuclear coincidence required for a tidal disruption event."}, {"argument": "The two-week detection with slow fading of about 0.5 mag in r and persistently blue g minus r near minus 0.1 mag matches a hydrogen-rich photosphere on a plateau. The real offset transient after deep non-detections to minus 25 days supports a massive-star explosion with weeks-long emission.", "hypothesis": "ii", "refuted_if": {"band": "ztfr", "day": 3, "direction": "below", "flux": 75, "why": "A Type II plateau should fade only hundredths of a magnitude per day so a fall to half the current flux in three days rules out a plateau."}, "weakest_point": "The Type II fit is poor with chi2 5.88 per point requiring peak 8 days before first detection at z 0.002 that implies an unphysically faint absolute magnitude near minus 11."}], "weights": {"ia": 0.8428, "null": 0.1, "tde": 0.0572}, "wording": {"term": "about as likely as not", "text": "It is about as likely as not that the object will be dimmer than our faint cutoff in green light 3 days from now.", "words_match": true}}, "prev": "5752231ef60ba41d92ca209f7dbfa64686d39564ce879bd9aa7